\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The normal hop is nilpotent}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{The \vocabk{slab operator}{fdip-01AE} is self-adjoint.
Each of its two directed normal hops has square zero.
On the spin and fiber at one site the ranges of #{P_-} and
#{P_+} both have dimension #{h=\dim_{\mathbb C}(S\otimes F)/2}.}

\proof{
\p{The grading commutes with the shifts and exchanges
#{P_{j,-}} with #{P_{j,+}}. Conjugating #{K_{L,U}} by
#{\Gamma} gives #{K_{L,U}^\dagger}, so
#{H_L(U)^\dagger=H_L(U)}. Its forward normal hop is
#{-a^{-1}\Gamma P_-V_U}. The shift preserves both normal
projector ranges. The hop therefore kills #{\im P_+} and
takes its values in that same space. Its square is zero.
The backward hop #{-a^{-1}\Gamma P_+V_U^\dagger}
kills #{\im P_-} and takes its values there. On each site
#{\Gamma} exchanges the two orthogonal ranges isometrically.
Their dimensions add to #{\dim_{\mathbb C}(S\otimes F)}.}

\p{Commutation with the links matters to self-adjointness.
For instance take one direction and two layers. Take spin
involutions #{\varepsilon_s^S,G} that anticommute, fiber involutions
#{A,B} that anticommute, all self-adjoint on spaces of dimension
#{2}. Such a pair acts by the sign and interchange of two
orthogonal lines. Give the slab #{\Gamma=G\otimes A} and normal
link #{B}. The link commutes with #{P_\pm} but anticommutes
with #{\Gamma}. The forward coefficient and the adjoint of the
backward coefficient then have opposite signs on #{\im P_-},
and neither vanishes there.}
}
